---
import QuestionLayout from '@layouts/QuestionLayout.astro';

const jsonLd = JSON.stringify({
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'FAQPage',
  mainEntity: [{
    '@type': 'Question',
    name: 'Why does model.execute return unauthorized_output when my host passes model evidence through?',
    acceptedAnswer: {
      '@type': 'Answer',
      text: 'The Spec 104 host-private output filter in the native executor rejects any connector output string that starts with / or contains ://. The full ModelRightsRecord that Spec 138 hosts return carries source_url values (https:// URLs), so forwarding it verbatim from a connector bridge into the catalog model.execute@1.0.0 guest fails with unauthorized_output (-4). As of 2026-10-29 the registry publishes model.execute@1.1.0 (registry#641, closed via registry#645), which defines a normative flat model_evidence shape: license_id, commercial_use, status, effective_usage, and an optional derivation without URLs. Hosts and bridges should emit exactly that shape. Forwarding the full nested ModelRightsRecord is non-conformant. Traverse v0.16.1 (Nov 3, 2026) changes the filter on every host (Spec 104 1.1.0 FR-008) so the signed model_evidence from traverse.model-runtime is no longer refused for holding public URLs, and ships Browser/Node activation (activateMediatedConnector) next to the native activate_exact_model_runtime binding, so no hand-written bridge is needed there. Swift, Android and .NET have no binding yet.',
    },
  }],
});

const relatedLinks = [
  { href: '/questions/what-is-the-model-execute-catalog-capability.html', label: 'What is the model.execute catalog capability?' },
  { href: '/questions/why-does-bundleembedder-fail-with-cannot-find-definition-for-import-connector-invoke.html', label: 'Why does BundleEmbedder fail on the connector_invoke import?' },
  { href: '/questions/what-is-model-usage-in-traverse.html', label: 'What is model_usage?' },
  { href: '/questions/how-do-hosts-trust-signed-models.html', label: 'How do hosts trust signed models?' },
  { href: '/blog/catalog-model-execute.html', label: 'Weekly demo: catalog model.execute' },
];
---
<QuestionLayout
  title="Why does model.execute return unauthorized_output when my host passes model evidence through?"
  description="Traverse's host-private output filter rejects strings with :// or a leading /, and the full ModelRightsRecord carries source URLs. model.execute@1.1.0 defines a flat model_evidence shape without URLs. v0.16.1 stops the filter refusing signed model_evidence and ships the binding."
  canonical="https://traverse-framework.com/questions/why-does-model-execute-return-unauthorized-output.html"
  category="Runtime and architecture"
  relatedLinks={relatedLinks}
  jsonLd={jsonLd}
>
  <p><strong>Short answer:</strong> the runtime is protecting the guest from host-private data, and the full rights record has URLs in it. Traverse's connector output filter rejects any string that starts with <code>/</code> or contains <code>://</code>. If your bridge forwards the host's whole <code>ModelRightsRecord</code> into <a href="/questions/what-is-the-model-execute-catalog-capability.html"><code>model.execute@1.0.0</code></a>, its <code>source_url</code> fields trip that filter and the call fails with <code>unauthorized_output</code> (<code>-4</code>).</p>

  <h2>Why the filter is there</h2>
  <p>A capability guest shouldn't see host paths or endpoints. That's part of the mediated connector rules in Spec 104: the host does the I/O, and only checked, host-neutral data crosses back into the WASM. Paths and URLs are the easiest leak to catch, so the native executor refuses them outright rather than guessing which ones are safe.</p>

  <h2>What the record looks like</h2>
  <p>Both Spec 138 hosts, native <code>traverse_runtime::exact_model</code> and the web <code>ExactModelBrowserHost</code>, return a nested record: model id, version and digest, a <code>rights</code> object (license, commercial use, attribution, redistribution, <code>source_url</code>, and a derivation block that can also carry a URL), plus <code>status</code> and <code>effective_usage</code>. The catalog contract's example shows a flat <code>{"{"} license_id, commercial_use {"}"}</code>, so the two don't line up exactly.</p>

  <h2>What to do now</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Emit the normative shape.</strong> Since 2026-10-29 the registry carries <code>model.execute@1.1.0</code> (<a href="https://github.com/traverse-framework/registry/issues/641">registry#641</a>, closed by <a href="https://github.com/traverse-framework/registry/pull/645">registry#645</a>). Its contract defines a flat <code>model_evidence</code> object: <code>license_id</code>, <code>commercial_use</code>, <code>status</code>, <code>effective_usage</code>, and an optional <code>derivation</code> with no URLs in it. Build exactly that in your bridge and leave the rest of the rights record on the host side.</li>
    <li><strong>Don't forward the full record.</strong> The 1.1.0 contract states that passing the nested <code>ModelRightsRecord</code> through is non-conformant under Spec 104, so <code>unauthorized_output</code> here is expected behavior, not a bug.</li>
    <li><strong>Same WASM as 1.0.0.</strong> 1.1.0 reuses the 1.0.0 artifact (same digest); what changed is the contract. The guest still copies whatever the host hands it, so byte-identical output across hosts depends on every host emitting the same shape.</li>
    <li><strong>Or upgrade to v0.16.1 and drop the bridge.</strong> See below.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Update, November 4, 2026: fixed in v0.16.1 for signed model evidence</h2>
  <p><a href="https://github.com/traverse-framework/traverse/releases/tag/v0.16.1">Traverse v0.16.1</a> (November 3, 2026) changes the filter on every host (Spec 104 1.1.0 FR-008, <a href="https://github.com/traverse-framework/traverse/pull/1770">traverse#1770</a>). It no longer refuses the signed <code>model_evidence</code> that <code>traverse.model-runtime</code> returns just because the rights record holds public URLs. Before, a real package failed with <code>connector_unavailable</code>. The filter still covers everything else a connector returns.</p>
  <p>v0.16.1 also ships the binding, so you don't write a bridge: native has <code>activate_exact_model_runtime</code> (since v0.16.0), and Browser and Node have <code>BundleEmbedder.activateMediatedConnector</code> with <code>exactModelMediatedConnector</code>. On the published v0.16.1 packages, <code>model.execute@1.0.0</code> returned the same output on native, Node and Chromium, including a <code>model_evidence</code> with the nested rights and its <code>source_url</code> fields (<a href="/questions/does-catalog-model-execute-run-on-published-traverse-packages.html">details</a>). Swift, Android and .NET don't have the binding yet. If you still forward the record through your own bridge on an older release, the advice above applies.</p>

  <h2>One more caveat</h2>
  <p>The contract declares <code>additionalProperties: false</code> on inputs, but on v0.15.0 the WASM still accepts an extra field and runs. Host-side enforcement (traverse#1658, PR #1671) shipped in Traverse v0.16.0 (November 2, 2026): unknown fields now fail with <code>invalid_input</code> before the artifact runs, on the native runtime path and the web <code>BundleEmbedder</code> when a component declares <code>contract_path</code>. It is one of the <a href="/questions/how-do-i-upgrade-to-traverse-v0-16-0.html">v0.16.0 breaking changes</a>.</p>
</QuestionLayout>
